{
 "profile_id": "uphongolo",
 "profile_name": "uPhongolo Local Municipality",
 "short_name": "uPhongolo",
 "_doc": "uPhongolo Local Municipality - 'TAX INVOICE/ STATEMENT', VAT REG NO 4000791873, account [number removed] (master list '01/[number removed]', site 'Pongola ETE & PO', 50/R_NEW REPUBLEC STREET, Pongola). SAME billing vendor and near-identical to Ditsobotla shape B, so the same traps apply.\nSERVICE SUB-HEADINGS on their own lines ARE present and reliable on all 10 bills: 'Electricity Basic', 'Electricity Consumption' (TWICE - once for the kVA demand register, once for the kWh register), 'Interest on Arrears', 'Rentals', and 'Property Rates' on 9 of 10 / 'Payments' on 1 of 10. Because the whole masthead + column header is REPEATED on page 2 and the service headings interleave across the page break, only ELECTRICITY is a boundary-forming section (it runs from the first 'Electricity Basic' to the end of the document); rates and sundry are 'overlay': true with 'end': '\\\\Z'. Every rule anchors on its own description text, which is unique per service, so nothing leaks across services.\nLedger columns: Date / Description / VAT / Debit / Credit / Balance. THE DEBIT COLUMN IS VAT-INCLUSIVE with the VAT printed separately, so ex-VAT is derived per line with a 'sub' compute. The meter table under each consumption row also prints the bill's OWN ex-VAT figure in its 'Exclusive' column, which is preferred over the derived one (they agree to the cent).\nMONEY IS COMMA-DECIMAL / SPACE-THOUSANDS ('46 106,70'). _to_decimal strips the comma as a thousands separator, which would make every figure 100x too big, so - following the Ditsobotla convention - every money pattern captures the RAND PART ONLY, '(-?\\\\d{0,3}(?:[ ]\\\\d{3})*),\\\\d{2}'. Each Rand figure therefore reads up to R0.99 LOW; the exact printed string is kept alongside in the parallel *_txt native fields (uph_kwh_inc_txt, uph_kwh_excl_txt, uph_vat_total_txt, ...).\nMeter table layout (wraps badly - never read the numeric columns positionally):\n  Meter Number Factor Date From Date To Band From Band End Rate Units Description  Reading Type Exclusive\n                1   15-05-2026 15-06-2026  0,00  9 999 999  1,82  22  22029KW - [434800 to 456829]  Actual  40 092,78\n   W50B/1                                        999,00         029,00\nThe METER NUMBER is on the line BELOW the data row (W50B/1 = kWh register, V50B/1 = kVA register) and the wide numeric columns (Band End, Units) wrap onto that line too. So kWh/kVA quantities and the register readings are taken from the DESCRIPTION text ('22029KW - [434800 to 456829]') and never from the Units column. Note the description says 'KW', not 'KWH' - '\\\\d+KW[ \\\\t]*-' also safely excludes '38KVA -'. On the LEDGER row the description wraps mid-bracket ('22029KW - [434800 to' / newline / '456829]'), so the readings are read off the meter-table copy, which is always on one line.\nSingle-band industrial supply (one kWh register + one kVA demand register, no TOU, no NMD): the single energy register is routed to the STANDARD band per the Ekurhuleni / KwaDukuza / Ditsobotla convention. Rule Zero: the bill prints no electricity-only ex-VAT subtotal, so total_ex_vat_r is the sum of its own parts and the built-in reconcile is therefore circular for this profile. The genuine checks are (a) the printed 'Exclusive' column against Debit-VAT per line, and (b) the printed whole-bill 'VAT Total' against the sum of the per-line VAT figures.\n'UPH_174_CustomerStatementReport 174 April.pdf' is only named differently - it is the SAME layout and the SAME billing data (it is the April 2026 statement, the month otherwise missing from the nine 'Statement-...' files). It is handled by the ordinary rules and is NOT rejected.\nTwo layout quirks worth knowing: the EFT PAYMENT ledger row sits ABOVE the first 'Electricity Basic' heading on 9 of 10 bills (so it is in the header, not in any section) and under a 'Payments' heading on page 2 of the July bill - the payment rules are therefore 'document' scope with collect:'sum'. And the interest row is printed BOTH as 'Interest [number removed]' and 'Interest: [number removed]' (with a colon), so the pattern allows an optional colon.",
